Investigation evidence chain — loggerd non-RK3588/HardwarePC SIGABRT
Run: loggerd-ka2-efficiency-20261029, build r10; investigation began at run revision 247.

Observed links

1. Original reproduction, approximately 2026-10-31 11:05 UTC. Capture: `../build/evidence/tests-r10-gap-legacy-pc-lifecycle-r245.log` (SHA-256 94e7c671fd1e7f103e31fa8f9e04b036f371600528167969a8c2b828741015cb); identical reproduction copy: `evidence/reproduction.log` (same SHA). The serial test built and launched the dedicated no-`__TICI__`, `-URK3588` loggerd/encoderd test targets on the KA2 host with the synthetic DMA-heap VisionIPC fixture. Preflight had no camerad/encoderd/loggerd and no users of any enumerated video/media/MPP/RGA node. The loggerd child was reported as returncode -6 (SIGABRT); encoderd initialized and qRoad FFmpeg output appeared. The output report shows segment 0 with multi-megabyte f/d/e video files, while Params stayed on segment 0 and no rotation report was published.

2. The test order is observable in `system/loggerd/tests/test_loggerd_liveness.py` (SHA-256 72c69e1e98cf2fdc841c51161e3d5dc0210b28035466e1798c4c4b4848d29342): each loop sends one synthetic frame to the fixture and corresponding camera-state/audio messages before `_wait_process_started(loggerd, ...)` checks `poll()`. The segment-0 files in link 1 independently show that loggerd processed some recording data. Therefore the original failure was after initial input/data output and before rotation, not before the first frame. This is an inference from the observed ordering and outputs, not a stack-based causal finding.

3. The original loggerd stdout captured in link 1 is empty. At that attempt the process soft core limit was 0; no core or backtrace was available. Debugger report `../build/reports/report_debug-hardware-pc-sigabrt.md` (SHA-256 fb3032117b10b15886070a4298358b199a14599d840d0b29d369447bf09d5430) agrees that the abort site was not identified and corrects the same frame-before-poll timeline.

4. Capture prerequisites were checked at 2026-10-31 11:16 UTC; record: `evidence/capture-environment.log` (SHA-256 5c92a95b4617b3c275692ddc7d01af2e30124ad79785b90ce15cc91be1d320d0). GDB 15.1 is available; the isolated loggerd test binary (SHA-256 94b304426795d8482ed6477c82f8946252fb3bd0f4dfd2226ef2a22c5c8062ff) has debug_info and is not stripped. A harmless `/bin/sh` SIGABRT control produced a GDB stack with the same command options. No kernel core pattern or global setting was changed; the investigation process soft core limit remained 0 to avoid core-file writes.

5. One guarded same-fixture GDB attempt ran at route `2026-10-31--11-13-24`; capture: `evidence/gdb-capture-attempt.log` (SHA-256 dcb497fc9153269319e76193c97a8f1f0712defd9d54ccd1bdfd432c321376fc). The temporary pytest `Popen` subclass matched only the loggerd executable path and invoked `/usr/bin/gdb -q -batch -ex 'set pagination off' -ex 'set debuginfod enabled off' -ex 'set print frame-arguments none' -ex 'handle SIGABRT stop print pass' -ex run -ex 'thread apply all bt 30' -ex continue --args /data/openpilot/build/system/loggerd/tests/loggerd_non_rk3588`. The runner marker and failure object's argv show GDB wrapped loggerd directly, not pytest. GDB logged thread initialization and detached one vfork child (PID 3979031) under its default follow-parent behavior. It did not emit `Program received signal SIGABRT`, a process termination marker, or a backtrace; the printed loggerd output ends mid-line before such a marker. The Python test consequently observed the GDB wrapper exit with returncode 0 at its existing loggerd liveness assertion. That is the GDB process status, not a confirmed status for its inferior.

6. The GDB attempt does not establish whether the loggerd inferior exited normally under instrumentation, whether the abort occurred in a detached child, or whether output capture ended before GDB's signal report. These remain competing possibilities, not explanations. Source `system/loggerd/loggerd.cc` (SHA-256 3a2e621adc50f44f727a12b0d8deb7410a4c502948ae2297069aa40b4ea8f5a9) contains several fatal assert sites in rotation, encoder-message handling, and subscription setup; the `video_writer.cc` warning observed during the GDB attempt is non-fatal at its call site and does not identify the SIGABRT. No one assert or subsystem is established as the cause.

7. The postflight record at 2026-10-31 11:16:30 UTC, `evidence/postflight.log` (SHA-256 cb08a34b0675ad226347a6dc9556e73882bb29d761fb112d56fe6d5f22fb536e), found no camerad/encoderd/loggerd/GDB process and no users of 83 camera/media/MPP/RGA nodes. The temporary wrapper and its bytecode were removed and absence verified; the instrumentation ledger is `evidence/instrumentation-ledger.md` (SHA-256 050601c005d77f898c2654e4400a2b8dbc14fde0120424202f85d7a93c002d85). No product source, test, SCons file, or global core setting was changed.

Conclusion boundary

Observed: an unwrapped loggerd process in the non-RK3588 test lifecycle was reported as SIGABRT after frame data was produced and before rotation; its output/core were unavailable. Observed: one direct GDB-wrapped lifecycle did not provide a SIGABRT marker or stack, and GDB detached one vfork child. Inference: the unknown abort remains somewhere in the loggerd lifecycle or an associated process; available evidence cannot distinguish them. No root cause is confirmed and no candidate fix has been tested.

Closure observation required: a complete SIGABRT backtrace from the loggerd inferior or from the aborting forked child, with its PID identified and its parent/child relation recorded. Any future capture requires a new owner authorization after this investigation handoff; no additional board launch was performed here.
